\title{Non-Archimedean Cancellation, Tropical Corner Loci,\\and Weighted Intersection Correspondence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate three elementary mechanisms forming a rigorous bridge from classical algebraic equations to tropical geometry. For a finite vanishing sum of nonzero elements in a non-Archimedean valued division field, the maximal valuation cannot occur uniquely. It follows that the valuation data of every zero of a finite sum lies in the max-corner locus of the associated tropical term family. Next, the corner locus of a real-valued tropical family is invariant under every positive common rescaling, so all positive integral scales agree exactly. Finally, a multiplicity-preserving bijection between finite classical and tropical intersection sets preserves weighted intersection numbers. Thus a classical B\'ezout count $de$ transfers whenever such a correspondence exists. We distinguish these results from stronger lifting and convergence theorems and discuss algorithms, applications, limitations, and extensions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Tropical geometry replaces nonlinear algebraic objects by polyhedral ones while retaining substantial information. Under the max convention, a finite polynomial becomes a maximum of affine-linear functions, and its tropical hypersurface is the locus where the maximum is attained at least twice. The central question is why the zeros of a classical polynomial should be related to this corner locus.

The local reason is non-Archimedean cancellation. A non-Archimedean valuation satisfies a strengthened triangle inequality: the valuation of a sum is bounded by the larger valuation of its summands. More strongly, a finite sum of terms all strictly smaller than a fixed nonzero term remains strictly smaller than it. A uniquely dominant term therefore cannot be cancelled by the remaining terms. Whenever a finite sum of nonzero terms vanishes, at least two terms share the maximal valuation.

Applied to the monomial terms of a polynomial, this places every classical zero on the tropical corner locus. A second bridge concerns scale: multiplying every tropical term by the same positive scalar preserves every comparison and hence the corner locus exactly. A third bridge concerns enumerative geometry: a finite bijection preserving local multiplicities preserves their sum. Consequently, once the geometric correspondence is known, a classical B\'ezout count transfers to the tropical setting.

Sections~2--6 develop these statements. Section~7 gives algorithms and examples; Sections~8--10 discuss applications, scope, and future work.

\section{Definitions and conventions}

\subsection{Non-Archimedean valuations}

Let $K$ be a division field and let $\Gamma_0$ be a nontrivial linearly ordered commutative monoid with zero.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{multiplicative non-Archimedean valuation} is a map $v:K\to\Gamma_0$ satisfying
\[
v(0)=0,\qquad v(1)=1,\qquad v(ab)=v(a)v(b),
\]
and
\[
v(a+b)\leq \max\{v(a),v(b)\}.
\]
We assume the separation property $v(a)=0$ if and only if $a=0$.
\end{definition}

In particular, $v(-a)=v(a)$. The additive convention instead has $w(ab)=w(a)+w(b)$ and $w(a+b)\geq\min\{w(a),w(b)\}$. Our results use the multiplicative max convention.

\subsection{Maximal terms and corners}

Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set, $X$ a set, and $F_i:X\to\Gamma$ a family valued in a linearly ordered set.

\begin{definition}
An index $i$ is \emph{maximal at $x$} if $F_k(x)\leq F_i(x)$ for every $k\in I$. The point $x$ is a \emph{max-corner point} if two distinct indices are maximal there. Equivalently, for some $i\neq j$,
\[
F_i(x)=F_j(x)=\max_{k\in I}F_k(x).
\]
The set of such points is the \emph{max-corner locus}.
\end{definition}

When $X=\mathbb R^n$ and the terms are affine, $F(x)=\max_i F_i(x)$ is convex and piecewise linear. Its corners are the cells where multiple affine pieces are active.

\begin{lemma}[Sign reversal]
Let $\Gamma$ be a linearly ordered additive commutative group with order compatible with addition. The minimum of the family $-F_i(x)$ is attained at least twice if and only if the maximum of $F_i(x)$ is attained at least twice.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Negation reverses order:
\[
-F_i(x)\leq-F_k(x)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad F_k(x)\leq F_i(x).
\]
Thus the minimizing indices of the negated family are precisely the maximizing indices of the original family.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Weighted intersection numbers}

\begin{definition}
For a finite intersection set $S$ and multiplicity $m:S\to\mathbb N$, the \emph{weighted intersection number} is
\[
I(S,m)=\sum_{p\in S}m(p).
\]
\end{definition}

The definition is independent of how the local multiplicities arise; they may be algebraic local intersection numbers, lattice determinants, or stable tropical weights.

\section{Non-Archimedean cancellation}

\begin{theorem}[No unique maximal valuation]
Let $S$ be finite and let $a_i\in K$ be nonzero for all $i\in S$. If
\[
\sum_{i\in S}a_i=0,
\]
then for every $i\in S$ there exists $j\in S$, $j\neq i$, such that
\[
v(a_i)\leq v(a_j).
\]
Consequently, the maximum of the valuations is attained at least twice.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $i$ and suppose instead that $v(a_j)<v(a_i)$ for every $j\neq i$. Since $a_i\neq0$, its valuation is nonzero. The strict finite-sum form of the non-Archimedean inequality gives
\[
v\left(\sum_{j\neq i}a_j\right)<v(a_i).
\]
The vanishing-sum identity gives $\sum_{j\neq i}a_j=-a_i$, whose valuation is $v(a_i)$, a contradiction. Thus a distinct $j$ has valuation at least $v(a_i)$. Choosing $i$ maximal shows that both attain the maximum.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The nonzero hypothesis allows strict estimates and ensures all supported terms participate. Zero terms may be removed. Laurent monomials with nonzero coefficients evaluated on an algebraic torus are automatically nonzero.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}
For the $p$-adic norm, if $a+b+c=0$ with all terms nonzero, one cannot have $|a|_p>|b|_p$ and $|a|_p>|c|_p$. The ultrametric inequality would imply $|b+c|_p<|a|_p$, while $b+c=-a$ gives equality.
\end{example}

\section{From zeros to tropical corners}

Let $T_i:X\to K$ be a finite nonempty family. Set
\[
f(x)=\sum_{i\in I}T_i(x),\qquad F_i(x)=v(T_i(x)).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Valuation-to-corner theorem]
Fix $x\in X$. If
\[
\sum_{i\in I}T_i(x)=0
\quad\text{and}\quad
T_i(x)\neq0\ \text{for all }i,
\]
then there are distinct $i,j\in I$ with
\[
v(T_i(x))=v(T_j(x))=\max_{k\in I}v(T_k(x)).
\]
Thus every such classical zero lies in the max-corner locus of the termwise valuation family.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $i$ attaining the maximum, which exists because $I$ is finite and nonempty. Apply the cancellation theorem to $a_k=T_k(x)$. It supplies $j\neq i$ with $v(T_i(x))\leq v(T_j(x))$. Maximality gives the reverse inequality, so both indices maximize.
\end{proof}

For a Laurent polynomial
\[
f(z)=\sum_{u\in A}c_u z^u,
\]
where $A\subset\mathbb Z^n$ is finite, $c_u\neq0$, and $z\in(K^\times)^n$, all terms are nonzero. In additive logarithmic coordinates, term valuations become affine functions of the valuation vector. Hence the theorem gives the forward hypersurface inclusion
\[
\val(V(f))\subseteq\Trop(f).
\]
It does not establish the reverse inclusion. Lifting every corner to a zero requires additional algebraic-closure, completeness, and nontriviality hypotheses together with a lifting argument.

\begin{example}[Tropical line]
For
\[
F_0(x,y)=0,\qquad F_1(x,y)=x,\qquad F_2(x,y)=y,
\]
the corner locus of $\max\{0,x,y\}$ is
\[
\{(x,0):x\leq0\}\cup\{(0,y):y\leq0\}\cup\{(t,t):t\geq0\}.
\]
Every zero of a corresponding three-term Laurent polynomial has valuation vector on this three-rayed graph.
\end{example}

\section{Positive scaling and exact stabilization}

Let $F_i:X\to\mathbb R$ be any family and let $(cF)_i(x)=cF_i(x)$.

\begin{theorem}[Positive-scale invariance]
For every $c>0$,
\[
\Corner(cF)=\Corner(F).
\]
Equivalently, a point is a max-corner of the rescaled family if and only if it is a max-corner of the original family.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Positive multiplication preserves and reflects order:
\[
cF_k(x)\leq cF_i(x)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad F_k(x)\leq F_i(x).
\]
The maximizing index set is therefore unchanged, as is the condition that it contain at least two indices.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Integral-scale stabilization]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
\Corner((n+1)F)=\Corner(F).
\]
Thus the sequence of corner loci over positive integral scales is constant.
\end{corollary}

This is an exact setwise interpretation of an infinite-scale limit: the target corner locus occurs at every positive scale. It concerns common rescaling of tropical terms. It does not by itself assert Hausdorff convergence of varying classical zero sets or logarithmic amoebas; those require a topology, normalization, and analytic estimates.

\section{Weighted correspondence and B\'ezout transfer}

Let $S$ and $T$ be finite sets, $\phi:S\to T$ a bijection, and let $m_S:S\to\mathbb N$ and $m_T:T\to\mathbb N$ be multiplicities.

\begin{theorem}[Weighted correspondence]
If $m_S(p)=m_T(\phi(p))$ for every $p\in S$, then
\[
I(S,m_S)=I(T,m_T).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Reindex the finite sum:
\[
\sum_{q\in T}m_T(q)
=\sum_{p\in S}m_T(\phi(p))
=\sum_{p\in S}m_S(p).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional tropical B\'ezout transfer]
Let two classical plane curves have degrees $d$ and $e$. Suppose their finite classical intersection set $S$ has multiplicities $m_S$ with
\[
I(S,m_S)=de.
\]
Let $T$ be a finite tropical intersection set with multiplicities $m_T$. If a bijection $\phi:S\to T$ satisfies $m_S(p)=m_T(\phi(p))$ for all $p$, then
\[
I(T,m_T)=de.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The weighted correspondence theorem gives $I(T,m_T)=I(S,m_S)$, and the assumed classical B\'ezout count is $de$.
\end{proof}

The theorem is deliberately conditional. Constructing the correspondence and proving local multiplicity preservation contain the geometric content. Classical B\'ezout also requires an appropriate proper projective setting, while tropical B\'ezout uses balanced curves and stable intersections. Once compatibility is known, however, no further global counting argument is needed.

When multiple classical points tropicalize to one tropical point, the natural replacement is the fiberwise condition
\[
m_T(q)=\sum_{p\in\phi^{-1}(q)}m_S(p).
\]
Summation over $q$ again preserves the total and is an important extension of the bijective case.

\section{Algorithms and demonstrations}

\subsection{Corner detection}

At a numerical point $x$, compute $M=\max_i F_i(x)$ and collect all indices with value $M$. The point is a corner exactly when at least two indices are collected. The procedure uses $O(m)$ time for $m$ terms and at most $O(m)$ output space. On a grid of $N$ points it costs $O(Nm)$, excluding term evaluation. Exact integer or rational values give an exact decision; floating-point tolerances provide only numerical evidence.

To test scale invariance, repeat with $cF_i(x)$ for $c>0$. Exact arithmetic guarantees the same maximizing indices.

\subsection{Prime-adic cancellation test}

For a nonzero integer $a$, define
\[
\ord_p(a)=\max\{k\in\mathbb N:p^k\mid a\},\qquad |a|_p=p^{-\ord_p(a)}.
\]
For nonzero integers summing to zero, compute all orders. Maximal norm corresponds to minimal order, and the cancellation theorem says that the minimum order occurs at least twice. If $A$ bounds the absolute values, repeated division costs $O(m\log_p A)$ operations for $m$ inputs.

For $p=2$ and terms $12,20,-32$, the orders are $2,2,5$, so the norms are $2^{-2},2^{-2},2^{-5}$. The maximal norm occurs twice.

\subsection{Weighted-count checking}

Given paired records $(p,\phi(p),m_S(p),m_T(\phi(p)))$, check uniqueness of source and target labels and equality of paired weights, then sum either side. Hash tables give expected $O(N)$ time and $O(N)$ memory. For weights $[1,2,1,2]$ matched to a permutation of the same weights, both totals are $6$, agreeing with $d e$ for $d=2$ and $e=3$.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Polyhedral localization.}
The valuation-to-corner theorem excludes zeros from every region where one monomial uniquely dominates. When valuations become affine functions, the remaining locus is polyhedral and often much simpler than the original equation.

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
Tropical hypersurfaces depend on order comparisons, not on a common positive unit. Changing logarithmic base or multiplying all tropical values by one positive constant leaves the corner locus fixed.

\paragraph{Separation of tasks.}
The conditional B\'ezout theorem divides the argument into geometric construction of a multiplicity-preserving correspondence and combinatorial transfer of the total. Once local compatibility is established, the global equality is forced.

\paragraph{Sparse computation.}
Only supported monomials participate. For sparse polynomials, the cost of corner detection depends on the number of terms rather than the number of lattice points in a dense degree box.

\section{Scope and limitations}

The forward hypersurface theorem proves necessity, not sufficiency: every suitable zero gives a corner, but lifting arbitrary corners requires deeper results. Positive-scale invariance concerns already tropical term families and is not an analytic convergence theorem for classical varieties. B\'ezout transfer assumes a finite multiplicity-preserving correspondence and does not construct it. Finally, nonzero term evaluations are essential to the stated cancellation result; ordinary polynomial evaluations on coordinate hyperplanes may require removing vanished terms, whereas Laurent monomials on a torus are nonzero automatically.

These boundaries identify the additional mathematics needed for stronger conclusions rather than obscuring it.

\section{Future directions}

Five extensions arise naturally.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Prove the reverse inclusion for Laurent hypersurfaces over algebraically closed, complete, nontrivially valued fields, lifting every max-corner point to a zero after an appropriate completion.
\item Establish Hausdorff convergence on compact polytopes of logarithmic zero sets under
\[
\operatorname{Log}_t(z)=\left(\frac{\log|z_1|}{\log t},\ldots,\frac{\log|z_n|}{\log t}\right)
\]
as $t\to\infty$.
\item Prove stable plane tropical B\'ezout: balanced tropical curves of degrees $d$ and $e$, with integral weights and no common component, have total stable intersection multiplicity $de$.
\item Replace pointwise bijection by fiberwise preservation: the sum of classical local multiplicities over each tropical point should equal its stable tropical multiplicity.
\item Extend product rules for corner loci from ordered additive groups to cancellative ordered additive commutative monoids when finite term families attain their extrema.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A uniquely dominant non-Archimedean term cannot disappear in a vanishing sum; valuation images of zeros therefore lie on tropical corners. Common positive rescaling preserves dominance and hence gives exact stabilization of corner loci. A multiplicity-preserving finite correspondence reindexes a weighted sum and transfers a classical intersection number, including the conditional B\'ezout value $de$.

These principles locate the universal algebraic, order-theoretic, and combinatorial components of the classical-to-tropical bridge while exposing the remaining geometric tasks: lifting corners, proving convergence, and constructing multiplicity-preserving correspondences.

\end{document}

